{
    "title": "Prior Balance Replacement Funds",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "November 24, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-24/pdf/2025-20831.pdf",
    "summary": "The Interior Department is cleaning up and updating its rules for abandoned mine land funding. It\u2019s removing references to an old, one-time funding stream that has already run its course and aligning the regulations with current law, including a higher annual transfer limit set by Congress.\n\nWhat\u2019s changing? The Office of Surface Mining Reclamation and Enforcement is deleting mentions of \u201cprior balance replacement funds,\u201d which were temporary dollars used to make up for earlier allocations that were never appropriated. Since those payments are finished, the references are now outdated. The rule also updates the annual cap on certain Treasury transfers from $490 million to $750 million, reflecting a change Congress already made in 2019. In addition, the agency tidies up language across several parts of its rulebook to improve clarity, including how \u201cminimum program\u201d dollars work so smaller State and Tribal programs are topped up to at least $3 million when they still face the most urgent coal-related hazards. These updates take effect January 23, 2026 unless significant adverse comments are filed by December 24, 2025.\n\nWho benefits? State and Tribal programs that manage abandoned mine land grants get clearer, simpler rules and fewer obsolete terms to navigate. Budget planners will see regulations that match the funding limits already in law, which should improve predictability. Smaller programs, in particular, may benefit from the clarified path to reach the $3 million minimum when they still have high-priority safety and environmental problems to fix. The agency also stresses there are no new reporting burdens or added costs, and independent reviews found no major economic or environmental impacts.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? The changes are mostly housekeeping, but some programs may need to update internal guidance and grant documents that referenced the old funding terms. In tight budget years when transfers bump up against the $750 million cap, certain payments may be reduced proportionally, which could complicate planning for some States and Tribes. And while any unspent funds from the old, one-time program can still be used, they remain governed by the rules in place when those grants were first awarded, which may add a layer of complexity.\n\nOverall, this is an administrative tune-up rather than a policy shift, aimed at making the rules match today\u2019s funding reality and removing clutter. If you have views on the changes, you can submit comments online by December 24, 2025.",
